Austin did some cool exploration on how to generate synthetic chain-of-thought (CoT) to train long context visual language models and provide clues from various pages. He also explored making this reasoning capability toggleable.

@further_ai: "I made a paper! Essentially: Extract evidence from pages and sort the top K to make a reasoning trace. Add in a control token and we can turn it on or off. Internalize with model merging."

The linked paper discusses internalized reasoning for long-context visual document understanding.

How do you beat a 235B model with 32B parameters on long-document understanding? You teach it to reason during training and internalize it at inference. Meet @LightOnIO OriOn-Qwen-SR1, which is open source.

@LightOnIO: "Introducing New SOTA Long Context VLM. LightOn OriOn-Qwen-SR1 reasons over full documents and executes it implicitly at inference. Reasoning is compressed into the model's weights, with no verbose output and no added latency. It achieves state-of-the-art results on MMLongBenchDoc, ahead of Qwen3 VL with seven times fewer parameters. Kudos to @further_ai for this new milestone! Reasoning starts at reading."

The linked resources provide more information about the model and its deployment capabilities.

SOTA retrievers typically rely on a large-scale contrastive pretraining phase, but that data is often closed source. Huge props to @antoine_chaffin and @raphaelsrty, who got tired of this and open-sourced the data for a SOTA retriever!

@antoine_chaffin: "Today we release all the data sources (and more) in one place, more than 1.4B query-document pairs. Plus a new high-quality web dataset built on FineWeb-Edu, replacing the outdated 'common crawl' splits most mixtures still rely on (thanks @orionweller)."

Open-sourcing such data can significantly enhance the accessibility and quality of training resources for AI models.

@LightOnIO is one of the last hopes for the information retrieval community. I still believe retrieval is not dead; it has potential beyond mere markdown management for agentic memories. They open-sourced two retrievers, LateOn and DenseOn, both with 149M parameters, under Apache 2.0, trained on identical data with different architectures.

The interesting finding comes when testing generalization. Dense retrieval squashes each document into one vector at index time, which is fast but lossy, as it discards information not captured in the compression. In contrast, late interaction (ColBERT-style) keeps a vector per token and matches token-to-token at query time, preserving more information but requiring more compute.

BEIR is the standard benchmark for evaluation, but it has a problem: training corpora overlap with evaluation corpora, which means scores measure familiarity rather than true retrieval quality. LightOn rebuilt the evaluation by stripping every document from BEIR that appeared in their training data. As a result, dense models dropped in rank under decontamination, while ColBERT models held or improved their positions, with an average delta of +2.01 for dense and +3.55 for multi-vector. This indicates that in niche, internal, or domain-specific corpora that the model has never seen, compressed dense vectors struggle, while late interaction continues to perform well.

I’ve never been this excited about search. Six to seven years ago, information retrieval (IR) saw an influx of paradigms enabled by the significant headroom created by MS MARCO and then BEIR. However, progress slowed down since then.

Today, Diane releases perhaps the most ambitious IR benchmark to date: OBLIQ-Bench. The queries in it are designed to be increasingly opaque to current first-stage retrieval paradigms, placing the bottleneck very early in the search process, as the relevance of a document to the query is quite latent. I can't wait for core IR research on fundamentally more powerful paradigms for first-stage search to be reignited again. Stay tuned for more stories about this, and read Diane's thread and her paper below!

@dianetc_: "We set out to build a better retriever, so we looked for the hardest IR benchmarks. For each, we asked how much headroom remained by running oracle reranking with a frontier LLM. Most had little room left! So we built OBLIQ-Bench to study much harder search queries than before."

Happy to see more omnimodal embedding love! I hope there'll be a report to read. Congrats on the release @JinaAI_!

@N1colAIs: "@JinaAI_ has hopped on the omnimodal train. They just dropped a collection of two Omni embedding models (0.9B & 2B). Similar to BidirLM, they seem to rely on the Qwen modality head for the larger one, while sticking with EuroBERT for the nano version."

The linked collection features multimodal embedding models that align text, image, video, and audio tasks.

Such a good poster! Thanks for resharing.

@matospiso: "For anyone interested, here's the poster we presented at ECIR Late Interaction Workshop, and a blog post describing the method in more detail."

The linked paper introduces SMVE, a fast and scalable approach to late-interaction retrieval based on sparse random projections.

LightOn and Weaviate folks are doing great work on late-interaction and multivector retrieval. Check it out if you're curious.

@weaviatepodcast: "Multi-Vector Search with Amélie Chatelain and Antoine Chaffin! This one covers: an introduction to multi-vector search, multi- vs. single-vector, comparison with cross encoders, ColGrep for coding agents, reasoning-intensive retrieval, multimodal multi-vector, the cost of multi-vector, MUVERA and PLAID, ColBERT-Zero, and PyLate."

Video by Weaviate vector database

So proud of this one. My first published research post! It introduces a family of rerankers that handle both text and document pages with a single adapter, leading all open models in its class on ViDoRe V3.

Thank you @AmelieTabatta for being the best collaborator through every ablation.

@AmelieTabatta: "Today, we release a collection of multimodal rerankers, handling both text and document images, powered by @coreprinciple_'s work. The 2B model outperforms other same-size open rerankers, achieving 62.66 NDCG@10 on ViDoRe V3. Here’s what we learned."

